SYNTHESIS
====================================================================================================
Most useful criteria in this audit:
  lrc_close_sector: tie_rate->safe_gap_count, mean_abs_spearman=0.881, tie_rate=0.689
  local_moat_min: origin_score_norm->origin_clearance, mean_abs_spearman=0.821, tie_rate=0.491
  phase_half: H->max_gap, mean_abs_spearman=0.790, tie_rate=0.000
  safe_deficit: origin_score_norm->origin_clearance, mean_abs_spearman=0.758, tie_rate=0.300
  local_moat_sum: origin_score_norm->origin_clearance, mean_abs_spearman=0.689, tie_rate=0.230

Operational conclusion:
  1. Half-turn phase H remains the global spread meter: low H is the
     unanchored open-semicircle signal, while high H often means many
     locally lonely vertices.
  2. Scalar rank gauges such as local_moat_min and safe_deficit are better
     marked LRC meters, but their H is mostly tie-path or transitive noise.
     Use the marked origin score and score histogram instead.
  3. Origin blocker relief is not a good clock-wide scalar by itself, but
     it is a useful selected-snapshot diagnostic for 'why is runner 0 not
     lonely?': read its strict/tie pattern before trusting its completed H.
  4. k2, threshold-deficit, and bracket-relief pressure gauges should be read
     by strict SCCs and directed triangles.  In the selected n=14/n=18 rows
     they still peel rather than forming a cyclic pressure core.

Proposed LRC tournament metric vector:
  (phase_H, phase_score_width, origin_marked_score, safe_deficit_score_hist,
   origin_blocker_score_width, pressure_largest_SCC, pressure_triangles,
   strict_tie_rate, edge_flip_rate)

This is the useful replacement for a single loneliness scalar: H supplies
the cyclic spread coordinate, while marked scores and pressure-core shape
supply the LRC threshold coordinate.
